The MIT License, in plain English

Copyright (c) Year 5000 Since the Creation of the World,
The Flying Spaghetti Monster

You may use this work for free.

You may copy it, change it, share it, put it together with other work,
publish it, give it away, or sell it. You may let other people do
all of these things too. You do not have to ask for permission.

There is just one condition: when you share this work, or a large
part of it, keep the copyright notice above and these permission
words with what you share. This way, other people can see who gave
them these freedoms.

The work is given to you as it is, with no promises. Its makers do
not promise that it will work, that it will suit your needs, that it
is fit for sale, or that it will avoid problems involving other
people's rights.

If you use it and something goes wrong, the people who made it or
hold its copyright are not responsible for paying for any loss,
damage, or other claim arising from the work or from using it.

These words express the same permission, notice condition,
no-promises rule, and no-liability rule as the MIT license,
but they are written for an ordinary reader instead of in
the standard legal wording.

R'amen.
